## Risks and fallback

The main risks are compute and clip quality; each has a planned response.

- **Compute.** Video diffusion is expensive, so clips stay short (16 frames) and small (64×64).
- **Quality.** Clips from a one-month, from-scratch model will likely be blurry. The analysis explains why.
- **Memorization.** With few lesion clips, the model may copy them. This is measured directly, not assumed away.
- **Medical validity.** A generated lesion only looks like one; it is not confirmed dysplasia without a clinician. This goes in the ethics section, together with patient privacy if the model copies real videos.
- **Fallback.** If real video proves too hard, the same model on Moving MNIST still answers the pretraining-vs-copying question.

Training the RT-GI detector on synthetic data is follow-up research, not part of this course project.

